1.Strings are immutable 

s = "aBCDEF"
s = "A" + s[1:]  
print(s)

     In this s variable is referred to the value "aBCDEF" but in second line due to the modification,it creates a new string and it is referenced by the same variable s. 
What about the first string and how to access them? Can't access them because if the value can't reference by any variable, then cant access.
Garbage collector will remove that old object. 
     What if need to keep two values: Use different variable name.

## Python cannot modify a string,so the operation creates new string.


2.String Methods:
   upper()  -  string.upper()
   lower()  -  string.lower()
   split()  -  string.split(separator)
   title()  -  string.title()
   capitalize()  - string.capitalize()
   join()   -   "separator".join(string)
   replace()  - string.replace(old,new)
   find()    -  string.find("substring")
   count()  -  string.count("substring")
   len()    -  len(string)
   startswith() - string.startswith("prefix")
   endswith() - string.endswith("suffix")
   strip()  - string.strip()
   casefold() - string.casefold() - It is like lower(), but stronger. It handles some special characters from different languages better.
   center() - string.center(width,"any character to fill the space")  - make the length of the string as given width and put the string in the center. Keep the extra space in right.
              But if the given width is less than length of string, then it just returns the original string.
   
